  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nIntensifying LLJ and associated theta-e surge yielding widespread \nconvection across central IN as expected this morning with a decent \nEML nosing into our SW CWA. Surface obs/mesoanalysis show frontal \nboundary and associated surface instability gradient currently well \nsouth of our area and strongest convection remains focused in IND's \nCWA. Still getting a few storms and moderate rain along more \nelevated portions of the front in our southern CWA where around 1000 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE still exists. These storms pose little/no severe risk \nbut will keep a close eye on rainfall amounts given how soaked that \nportion of our CWA is. All of this activity should wind down shortly \nafter sunrise as the LLJ weakens and midlevel lapse rates stabilize \nsomewhat. The rest of the day should remain mostly dry as midlevel \nridge builds slightly over our area with general AVA. Still, the \nairmass remains moist and there are some hints of a weak MCV \narriving late in the day. These two factors could support some \nisolated convection during the afternoon and evening but coverage \nand impacts should remain very low. Flood watch currently runs until \n15Z but may be able to cancel a few hours early if convective trends \nbehave as expected.\n\nUnfortunately, active weather will continue through the weekend. \nStalled frontal boundary plaguing our area all week will lift north \nas a warm front given exiting eastern Canada upper low and eastward \nejection of what could be a very potent MCV out of the Central \nPlains. Mesoscale details are still somewhat unclear given a high \ndegree of spread among various models. However, the overall synoptic \nstage is set for potential severe weather somewhere in the region. \nAnother surge of the LLJ is expected, pushing at least moderate \ninstability into the area while 0-6km bulk shear values remain \nstrong enough on the NE fringes of this ridge to support organized \nconvection. One wild card is that convection will likely be ongoing \nduring the morning and that could limit instability during the \nafternoon. Hi-res CAM's are split on whether we will get organized \nredevelopment later in the day. If organized convection can develop \nthen straight-line winds would be the main concern, but hail and \ntornadoes can't be ruled out. Luckily, best chances for storms are \ncurrently leaning toward our northern CWA which can handle more rain \nbetter than our south. These storms will also be more progressive \nwith less potential training/backbuilding. Will obviously have to \nwatch the hydro aspect given our water-logged south but overall \ndoesn't appear to be a huge flooding risk at this point.\n\nThe front returns south as a cold front on Sunday. Once again, \nsevere weather is a possibility given moderate levels of MLCAPE and \ndeep layer shear. A lot will depend on the exact timing with the \npotential for an early afternoon frontal passage that limits \ninstability for most of our CWA and focuses better severe chances \njust to our S/SE. Still plenty of time for refinements to this \nforecast though. Thankfully, quiet weather finally returns Sunday \nnight and persists through at least Tuesday.",
